.TH USBRADIOPLUS-TUNE 8 "November 2026" "USBRadioPlus alpha" "System Administration"
.\" source-sha256 rust/tune/src/catalog.rs e53b9a2a35c5251d7039f5f52ce1d9f1ad31b6bdc12ec081d80cadfb52632c47
.\" source-sha256 rust/tune/src/control.rs 5062a2064f466dc687f6ecce3f07fcce76f499c0d17ffbee8881ed25eba97cad
.SH NAME
usbradioplus-tune \- tune and monitor a shared radio interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B usbradioplus-tune
.br
.B usbradioplus-tune
[-n node] [--backend asterisk|standalone] [--socket path]
.br
[--check] [--offline] [--config path] [--lock path]
.SH DESCRIPTION
This keyboard-operated utility provides the same radio and processing menus
for the ASL3 channel and the standalone service. The default backend is
Asterisk. Run it as root on an ASL3 node. Use
.B -n
to select a named channel before opening the menu; otherwise the active channel
is selected. If Asterisk cannot report an active channel, the utility falls
back to the first named channel in the configuration file.
.PP
For standalone operation, install the independent
.B usbradioplus-tune
package and run
.BR "sudo usbradioplus-tune --backend standalone --node NODE" .
It requires no Asterisk or channel package. Root and the service account can
access the service's private local socket; file edits additionally require
ordinary write access to the configuration file and its directory.
The tuner asks the service for its fixed canonical configuration path before
editing. An explicit
.B --config
must resolve to the same file. Native mode never creates an Asterisk sample.
.PP
Each edit is written atomically. The following controls are safe to apply with
a processing reload: all Local receiver, Linked audio, and Voice + telemetry
graph settings; all
.BR [receive] ,
.BR [transmit] ,
.BR [ctcss] ,
.BR [dcs] ,
and
.B [diagnostics]
settings; and
.BR hardware_input_gain_db ,
.BR hardware_output_a_gain_db ,
.BR hardware_output_b_gain_db ,
.BR hardware_output_a_assignment ,
.BR hardware_output_b_assignment ,
.BR hardware_deemphasis_corner_hz ,
and
.BR hardware_preemphasis_corner_hz .
.PP
In Asterisk mode, the utility restarts Asterisk for every other edit that changes channel or
hardware construction: all
.B [asterisk]
and
.B [duplex]
controls;
.BR channel_enabled ;
every named-channel
.B *_profile
selector; and all remaining
.B [hardware]
controls. The latter includes device identifier, serial number, interface
type, EEPROM choice, PTT polarity, repeater signaling metadata, clipping LED,
and GPIO or parallel-port assignments.
Standalone edits apply through the running service; a rejected generation
restores the preceding file and attempts to reapply it. Successful tuner live
updates retain temporary calibration keying until the frontend releases it.
An external service reload or shutdown ends the maintenance session.
Automatic calibration changes live radio state and saves adjusted file settings
as it applies them. Use the explicit
.B Write live radio tuning and EEPROM values
action to save current live tuning to interface EEPROM when enabled. A session
Save only establishes a file restore point.
.PP
Menus and setting editors identify the configuration file and section that
will receive edits. A missing selected profile produces a warning and uses
the channel-named section, or the flat defaults when that section is absent.
Edits to flat defaults affect every channel that inherits those settings.
Calibration reports missing-profile fallbacks before changing live state.
Device swaps are rejected if they would change another channel's device identity.
.PP
Menu buttons use consistent action names. Select opens the highlighted item,
Apply commits an edit, Back returns one menu level, Exit leaves the utility,
Cancel abandons an edit, and Close dismisses information without changing it.
Every menu returns focus to the item last selected. Choice editors place focus
on the current value, and numeric and text editors announce the current value
and units before accepting input. These conventions permit efficient keyboard
operation with a screen reader and do not require a mouse.
.TP
.B Select named-channel profiles
Choose the profiles for the selected radio identity. Asterisk mode offers the
documented
.BR asterisk_profile ,
.BR hardware_profile ,
.BR receive_profile ,
.BR transmit_profile ,
.BR ctcss_profile ,
.BR dcs_profile ,
.BR duplex_profile ,
.BR diagnostics_profile ,
.BR local_profile ,
.BR link_profile ,
and
.BR voice_telemetry_profile
selectors for the selected named radio channel. For each selector, the picker
offers only existing scoped sections of that type. Use
.B Use channel default
to remove the explicit selector and restore the documented implicit profile
whose name matches the channel. A profile-selector edit restarts Asterisk in
Asterisk mode. Standalone mode offers applicable shared selectors without
.B asterisk_profile
and applies edits through the service.
.SH CM119 AND SIGNALING MENUS
The CM119 hardware menu groups USB identity, gain, receiver, transmitter,
CTCSS, DCS, GPIO and parallel-port controls. USB-device swapping and
Asterisk-owned signaling metadata are available only in Asterisk mode.
The receiver and
transmitter submenus edit the corresponding
.B [receive]
and
.B [transmit]
configuration. The CTCSS and DCS submenus edit their separate signaling
sections. Select one signaling method per direction: carrier, CTCSS, or DCS.
Receive CTCSS is independent of transmit signaling: it requires a CTCSS
receive method, a CTCSS source other than no indication, and a supported
receive-frequency list. Transmit CTCSS is likewise independent of receive
signaling: it requires a transmit CTCSS method and a supported transmit default
tone. A receive-to-transmit frequency map is required only when both directions
use CTCSS; then the receive and transmit lists must have equal length and map
by position. Before selecting DCS, configure the corresponding receive or
transmit code.
See
.BR usbradioplus.conf (5)
for accepted values and routing rules.
.PP
CTCSS and DCS each have independent direct PCM peak dBFS controls. Hardware
output gain is applied after either generated waveform. DCS uses fixed spectral
shaping and bypasses transmitter pre-emphasis and all speech processing controls.
Its optional 134.4 Hz end-of-transmission tail tone is sent before PTT release
for 150 through 200 milliseconds; the default duration is 180 milliseconds.
.TP
.B Automatic RX noise and squelch calibration
With no carrier present, this adjusts CM119 capture gain and reports peak and
RMS measurements in PCM codes and dBFS. Flat discriminator calibration targets
27,000 peak PCM codes within 2,750 codes and makes at most 48 attempts. When
DSP carrier squelch is selected, it also derives and applies a squelch starting
point. A warning identifies de-emphasized or inadequate discriminator noise.
.TP
.B Automatic RX voice calibration
With a steady 1 kHz signal at 3 kHz deviation, this adjusts the local receiver
input gain. Use a calibrated signal at the receiver input.
The reference target is 7,200 peak PCM codes within 360 codes.
.TP
.B Automatic RX CTCSS calibration
With the configured CTCSS signal present, this adjusts only the CTCSS decoder
input gain. It does not change voice gain.
The reference target is 2,400 peak PCM codes within 100 codes.
.TP
.B Flash transmitter
Send three one-second PTT and calibrated 1 kHz tone bursts separated by
1.5 seconds. Use it only into a dummy load or on an authorized test frequency.
Press Enter to stop. Completion, cancellation and failures attempt final
unkey and test-tone cleanup.
.TP
.B Adjust TX voice output with a sustained 1 kHz tone
Key the transmitter with the calibrated 1 kHz source while editing the hardware
gain of the first output assigned to voice. The tone stops and PTT releases
after the edit is applied or cancelled.
.TP
.B Adjust TX CTCSS deviation while transmitting
Key the transmitter with the configured default CTCSS tone while editing
.BR transmit_peak_dbfs .
This requires CTCSS transmit signaling and an output assigned to CTCSS. The
tone stops and PTT releases after the edit is applied or cancelled.
.TP
.B Adjust auxiliary output with a sustained 1 kHz tone
Key the transmitter with the calibrated 1 kHz source while editing the hardware
gain of the first output assigned to auxiliary voice. The tone stops and PTT
releases after the edit is applied or cancelled.
.TP
.B Echo mode
Available only in Asterisk mode. The Local receiver menu enables or disables legacy echo playback through the
8 kHz Asterisk path. Disable echo when the test is complete. Hardware local
repeating (duplex3) remains a separate hardware mixer control, not a software
echo route.
Standalone mode exposes neither echo nor hardware local-repeat controls.
.SH STANDALONE RADIO SETTINGS
Radio values inherit from
.B [radio]
and node-specific
.B [radio NODE]
sections in the service configuration. Explicit shared hardware/signaling
profiles and named processing selectors retain the common resolver's
inheritance, validation and missing-profile warnings. Menus show the actual
radio or profile section receiving each edit.
.PP
Standalone defaults remain exact device selection, mono playback, output A
carrying voice, output B carrying tone, transmit pre-emphasis off, and EEPROM
disabled. Receive and transmit CPU savers are independent:
.B receive_cpu_saver_enabled
and
.BR transmit_cpu_saver_enabled .
Configured parallel inputs and outputs use the same hardware owner as USB GPIO;
no parallel transport opens when none is configured. Receive delay remains
standalone controller policy.
.PP
Enable native EEPROM explicitly with
.B hardware_eeprom_enabled=yes
in [radio] or [radio NODE].
A valid enabled image overrides capture gain, output A/B gains, transmit CTCSS
level and squelch threshold before startup preparation. Invalid or unreadable
images leave file tuning in effect. Startup never writes EEPROM.
.SH PROCESSING MENUS
The Local receiver, Linked audio, and Voice + telemetry menus edit their
separate processing chains. Each Stages menu enables or disables the available
stages and edits the order of equalizer, expander, AGC, de-esser, compressor,
and limiter. RNNoise, local receiver filters, the final limiter, and its
fixed transmitter band-pass are not reorderable. The local chain alone offers RNNoise
and CTCSS filtering; the voice/telemetry chain alone offers the final limiter
and fixed transmitter band-pass.
.PP
The Compressor and Limiter pages each select single-band or three-band mode.
Three-band mode uses low, mid, and high controls around configurable
crossovers. The Equalizer and De-esser pages expose their respective parameters.
The AGC page exposes its full-program LUFS target and 400-3000 ms loudness
window (default 3000 ms), gain rates and limits, separate raw dBFS activity
gate, hold, and deadband. A complete initial window precedes gain changes;
renewed activity preserves its history. Boosts also require the configured
hold. AGC does not buffer program audio or provide a
true-peak ceiling. See
.BR usbradioplus.conf (5)
for parameter definitions and ranges.
.TP
.B Show processing measurement snapshot
Show one coherent, recently published processing-statistics snapshot. It can be
briefly stale rather than waiting for native or link rendering.
.TP
.B Show complete active configuration
Show the selected radio's resolved configuration and live state.
.TP
.B Continuous status and RX/TX audio meters
Display COS, CTCSS, PTT, and receive/transmit peak, RMS, and clipping status
at one-second refresh intervals until Enter is pressed. Both backends use the
same frontend display and physical snapshot semantics.
.TP
.B Save this live processing session
Mark the current, already-applied configuration as the session restore point.
.TP
.B Write live radio tuning and EEPROM values
Ask the shared maintenance owner to save current live tuning to the interface
EEPROM when storage is enabled. Calibration edits already save their adjusted
file values. Native mode reports an error when EEPROM storage is disabled.
.TP
.B Discard live changes since the last save
Restore the configuration from the session restore point.
.TP
.B Create a timestamped configuration backup
Copy the current configuration to a timestamped adjacent backup file.
.SH MENU BEHAVIOR
In Asterisk mode, an absent configuration is created from an uncompressed
shipped sample. Standalone mode requires the service's existing configuration.
Controls are grouped by source and signaling system; configure a
signaling system before selecting it for either direction.
The local Filters page controls the receive band-pass and one PL-removal mode:
disabled, high-pass, or automatic decoded-tone notch. The voice/telemetry
Filters page controls the fixed transmitter band-pass. The link chain has no fixed
filter page.
.PP
Compressor and Limiter pages select single-band or three-band operation.
Single-band mode shows full-band controls. Three-band mode shows low/mid and
mid/high crossovers and separate low-, mid-, and high-band controls. Inactive
mode controls are retained for later use. The utility validates entered ranges
and relationships; the selected host validates the active graph against its current
sample rate on reload.
.PP
Each accepted configuration edit is written atomically and reloaded for live
evaluation. If validation or reload fails, the preceding file and active
settings are restored. Save sets the session restore point. Discard restores
that point, including when leaving the utility.
.TP
.B -n node
Select the active RadioPlus channel before opening either tuning interface.
.TP
.B --check
Check configuration storage and required executables. Online standalone checks
also contact the private local socket and verify the service-owned file path.
.TP
.B --offline
With --check, do not require Asterisk or the terminal menu package. This is
intended for installation and package tests.
.TP
.B --backend asterisk|standalone
Choose the host composition. The default is Asterisk; standalone operation
uses only the service's private local Unix socket.
.TP
.B --socket path
Use another standalone Unix socket. The default is
.BR /run/rpt-advanced/radio-control.sock .
The service owns this mode-0600 socket beneath a mode-0700 runtime directory.
The tuner control transport has no TCP listener.
.TP
.B --config path
Select the file for Asterisk or offline checks. During live standalone
operation it must resolve to the service's fixed canonical configuration file.
.TP
.B --lock path
Use another menu lock file.
.SH SERVICE-MONITOR PROCEDURE
Use a dummy load or authorized test frequency. Set capture gain first with an
unmodulated receiver and verify that ADC rail counts remain zero. Apply a
calibrated 1 kHz signal at system deviation and set recovered RX voice level.
Apply the configured CTCSS tone at the specified tone deviation and verify
decode margin.
.PP
Key into a service monitor, set voice peak deviation, then set CTCSS deviation.
Verify peak and RMS level, clipping, occupied bandwidth, PTT operation, and
tone turn-off behavior before saving. Repeat checks after reconnecting USB and
after restarting the selected radio host.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/asterisk/usbradioplus.conf
Named radio channels, hardware settings, saved tuning, and processing graphs.
.TP
.B /run/lock/usbradioplus-tune.lock
Asterisk-mode single-instance lock.
.TP
.B /etc/rpt_advanced/rpt_advanced.conf
Usual standalone configuration; the running service supplies the actual path.
.TP
.B /run/rpt-advanced/radio-control.sock
Service-owned private native maintenance socket.
.TP
.B /run/rpt-advanced/tune.lock
Standalone single-instance lock in the service-owned runtime directory.
